2026-09-29T21:07:19.063460Z [] {'source_reference_id': '12873300325', 'source_id': 'buynsw', 'source_url': 'https://buy.nsw.gov.au/prcOpportunity/4CF23D43-D9DB-4886-81C4EB4F11D069C6', 'title': 'Vivid Sydney ? Accessibility Advisor 2027 & 2028', 'issuing_agency': 'Destination NSW', 'category': 'Request for Quote', 'status': None, 'publish_date': '2026-09-24', 'closing_date': '2026-10-08', 'value_amount': None, 'value_currency': 'AUD', 'value_notes': 'Costs to be provided by respondent in costing template. Panel member fees are calculated at $100 per hour, per Panel member (ex GST), with an allowance of 20 hours per Panel member.', 'location': 'Sydney CBD, Circular Quay & The Rocks, Barangaroo, Darling Harbour, Other Vivid Sydney event locations', 'description': "Destination NSW is seeking a contractor to provide end-to-end accessibility advisory, program administration, and onsite support services for Vivid Sydney 2027 and 2028. The scope includes providing strategic and operational accessibility advice, reviewing event documentation, advising on accessibility initiatives and infrastructure, and planning and facilitating the Vivid Sydney Accessibility Advisory Panel. The contractor will also coordinate onsite accessibility assessments, conduct pre-event site visits, identify and escalate accessibility issues, and support the planning and deployment of accessibility-related signage across various Sydney locations including the CBD, Circular Quay, The Rocks, Barangaroo, and Darling Harbour. The services are required for the planning, pre-event, event delivery, and post-event periods.\n\nThe Project Management Fee component covers up to 25 working days for advisory and program administration, and up to 10 working days for onsite support. This was clarified via an addendum, correcting an earlier discrepancy. The Accessibility Advisory Panel will consist of up to six members, with fees calculated at $100 per hour (ex GST) per member for an allowance of 20 hours each, covering meetings, debriefs, and onsite assessments. The contractor is responsible for administering Panel member engagement, including contracting, invoicing, payments, and coordinating access and travel requirements. The contractor must provide their own equipment and cover most operational costs.\n\nThe contract period is for Vivid Sydney 2027 (event dates 28 May ? 19 June 2027), with an option for Vivid Sydney 2028 (dates to be confirmed). The Request for Quotation (RFQ) was published on 24 September 2026, with clarification requests due by 3:00pm on 1 October 2026, and responses closing at 3:00pm on 8 October 2026. The contract is expected to commence on Monday, 2 November 2026. Mandatory requirements include holding Public and Product Liability insurance of $20 million, Professional Indemnity insurance of $10 million, and Workers Compensation as required by law. Respondents must also comply with NSW Procurement Board Directions, Policies, Codes, Standards, and Modern Slavery Laws.\n\nThe successful contractor will enter into Destination NSW's Contract of Engagement, which stipulates that ownership of all intellectual property created under the contract will vest in Destination NSW. The contract also includes a 30-day termination for convenience clause for Destination NSW and prohibits the use of Artificial Intelligence Systems to develop, train, or direct AI with Destination NSW's intellectual property or confidential information without prior written consent.", 'summary_headline': 'Destination NSW seeks an Accessibility Advisor for Vivid Sydney 2027 and 2028.', 'contact_name': 'Ezzie Magrin', 'contact_email': 'ezzie.magrin@dnsw.com.au', 'contact_phone': None, 'lodgment_address': 'Buy NSW website (www.buy.nsw.gov.au)', 'documents': [{'file_name': 'VS27-Request-for-Quotation---Accessibility-Advisor.pdf', 'file_type': 'pdf', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/RFQ-12873300325/6648400de81e575a-VS27-Request-for-Quotation---Accessibility-Advisor.pdf', 'extracted_text': " \nRequest for Quote \na \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n \n \nDestination NSW \nRequest for Quote \nGoods & Services \n12873300325 \nVivid Sydney ? Accessibility Advisor 2027 & 2028 \nIssue Date: 24 September 2026 \nClosing Date: 8 October 2026 \nClosing Time: 3pm \n \n\n \nRequest for Quote \n2 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nConditions of Quotation \n1 \nIntroduction \n1.1 \nThis Request for Quote (RFQ) is issued by The Crown in right of the State of New South Wales, acting through \nthe Destination NSW (DNSW). \n1.2 \nDNSW seeks responses from suppliers who wish to supply the Goods/Services as described below in the \nStatement of Requirements. \n1.3 \nFor the purposes of this RFQ, all inquiries should be directed to the Contact Officer. \nContact Name: Ezzie Magrin \nContact Email:  ezzie.magrin@dnsw.com.au  \n1.4 \nAny information given to a respondent to clarify any aspect of this RFQ will also be given to all other \nrespondents if in the opinion of DNSW it would be unfair not to do so. \n2 \nEligibility of Response \n2.1 \nResponses must be submitted by a legal entity or, if a joint response, by legal entities, with the capacity to \nenter into a binding agreement. DNSW will only enter into an Agreement with such legal entity or entities. \n2.2 \nDNSW will not enter into an Agreement with a company that does not have an Australian Business Number \nand is not registered for GST. Normally, respondents must be registered for GST and state their ABN in their \nresponse. \n3 \nResponse Preparation \n3.1 \nBefore submitting its response, a respondent must: \na) \nExamine all information relevant to the risks and contingencies and other circumstances having an \neffect on its Response; and \nb) \nSatisfy itself: \ni) that the response is accurate, true and correct; and \nii) that the response is financially and practically viable for the respondent. \n3.2 \nThe respondent acknowledges that DNSW will not be liable to it for any expenses or costs incurred by it as a \nresult of its participation in this RFQ, including where the RFQ has been discontinued. \n3.3 \nRespondents should notify the Contact Officer in writing on or before the closing date and closing time if \nthey find any discrepancy, error or omission in this RFQ. \n3.4 \nIf, for any reason DNSW, at its sole discretion, requires the RFQ to be amended before the Closing Date and \nClosing Time, an addendum will be issued. \n\n \n \nRequest for Quote \n3 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n4 \nResponse Process \n4.1 \nThe Response must comprise of a completed quotation below and any attachments as may be necessary. \nAny attachments should be labelled to identify the section of the RFQ to which they relate. \n4.2 \nResponses must be fully received by the Closing Date and Closing Time.  \n4.3 \nDNSW may, in its discretion, extend the Closing Date and Closing Time. \n4.4 \nA Response must be lodged via Buy NSW website.  \n4.5 \nLate responses will not be considered, except where DNSW is satisfied that the integrity and competitiveness \nof the process is not compromised. DNSW shall not penalise any interested party whose response is received \nlate if the delay is due solely to mishandling by DNSW. \n4.6 \nRespondents must disclose any conflicts of interest. \n4.7 \nIf DNSW becomes aware of improper conflict of interest by a successful respondent after the Agreement has \nbeen executed, then DNSW reserves the right to terminate this Agreement. \n5 \nEvaluation Process \n5.1 \nResponses will be assessed against the evaluation criteria listed in the table below, which are not indicated in \norder of significance or given equal weight. \nPass / Fail Criteria  \n \na) Compliance with applicable NSW Procurement Board Directions, Policies, \nCodes and Standards \nb) Conflict of Interest \nc) Compliance with Proposed Agreement \nd) Commitment to Insurance Requirements \ne) Compliance with Modern Slavery Laws \nf) Acknowledges and complies with the NSW Supplier Code of Conduct \ng) Eligibility of Response (Refer to Section 2) \nh) Current Legal Proceedings \ni) Insolvency \nj) Acknowledgement and acceptance of Conditions of Quotation and Statement of \nRequirements \nk) Lodged by closing time \nl) Submitted as per the prescribed response forms \nNon-Price Criteria \n \na) Demonstrated experience in providing accessibility and inclusion advisory \nservices for major events, festivals or comparable complex public environments ? \n25% \nb) Demonstrated capacity to deliver the specifications, resources and services \nwithin the timeframes required, key considerations and general items outlined in \nthe scope ? 25% \nc) Human resource capacity including qualifications, skills, experience and \nproposed extent of the respondent?s key personnel involvement including sub-\ncontractors ? 20% \n\n \n \nRequest for Quote \n4 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nPrice Criteria \n \na) Fees and Charges ? 30% \n5.2 \nInformation supplied by the respondent will contribute to the assessment against each criterion.  \n5.3 \nDNSW may seek to clarify any information provided by a respondent. \n5.4 \nDNSW may in its discretion, and as part of the evaluation process, invite any or some of the respondents to \nan interview process, ask them to make personal presentations, or conduct site inspections regarding its \nresponse. The respondent shall undertake this at its own cost. \n5.5       Destination NSW may cull, set aside, or exclude a Response from further evaluation in which \nDestination NSW has evaluated and documented the Response as:  \n? \nnon-compliant, non-conforming or has not met the mandatory requirements of the Request;  \n? \nfailing to satisfy one or more of the evaluation criteria or criterions by scoring below a pass percentage of \n50%;   \n? \nnot ranked amongst the top-ranked Responses or is ranked as not being an economically advantageous \nResponse to which the Destination NSW?s tender evaluation team decide not to shortlist for further due \ndiligence exercises, or include in a shortlisting;  \n? \nan operational risk or financial risk or reputational risk or legal risk or work health and safety risk to \nDestination NSW as part of its evaluation, and/or as noted as part of due diligence exercises or accepting \nthe Response \n6 \nDisclosure Information \n6.1 \nFollowing DNSW?s decision, all respondents will be notified in writing of the outcome of their RFQ \nResponses. \n7 \nComplaints Procedure \n7.1 \nIt is the NSW Government?s objective to ensure that industry is given every opportunity to win Government \ncontracts. Should any entity feel that it has been unfairly excluded from responding or unfairly \ndisadvantaged by the Conditions in the RFQ, or the Statement of Requirements, it is invited to write to:  \nExecutive Director, Corporate Services, CFO and CPO \nDestination NSW \n88 Cumberland Street,  \nThe Rocks NSW  2000 \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nRequest for Quote \n5 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nStatement of Requirements \n1 \nBackground and Overview of Requirements  \nDestination NSW is the lead agency, champion and voice for the visitor economy within the NSW Government. Vivid \nSydney, the Southern Hemisphere?s leading multi-artform festival, transforms Sydney into a vibrant hub of creativity, \ninnovation and community connection. Proudly owned by Destination NSW, Vivid Sydney showcases NSW?s cultural \nvibrancy and positions it as a global leader in artistic innovation. For more information visit: www.vividsydney.com.  \nDestination NSW seeks the services of a Contractor or Contractors to coordinate and deliver event Accessibility \nAdvisory Services for Vivid Sydney 2027 & 2028. \nThe Destination NSW Contract of Engagement template is attached for review as part of this RFQ.   \nWhen responding to this RFQ, the Respondent should also consider the terms and conditions in this template and \nconfirm in writing that the terms and conditions can be met by the Respondent.   \nShould the Respondent wish to negotiate any part of the terms and conditions, those terms and conditions should be \nspecifically identified in the response to this RFQ. \nDestination NSW may elect to engage with the successful Contractor on specific elements of this request for \nquotation, but not others due to ongoing operational variations. Destination NSW may also elect to contract with \nmore than one Contractor for the services (or part thereof).  This election will be at the sole discretion of Destination \nNSW and the Contractor should indicate in its response to this RFQ whether it is willing to accept a contract for part, \nbut not all of the services set out below. \n2 \nScope  \nVivid Sydney \nThe Contractor will work collaboratively with the Vivid Sydney Event Operations Manager, and relevant Vivid Sydney \nteams to plan, implement and delivery the Vivid Sydney Accessibility Program.    \nThe Contractor will be responsible for providing specialist accessibility advisory services, planning and facilitating the \nVivid Sydney Accessibility Advisory Panel, coordinating and undertaking onsite accessibility assessments, and \nproviding dedicated Accessibility Program Officer/Administrator support. \nThe Contractor will work collaboratively with Destination NSW, Accessibility Advisory Panel members, relevant \nsuppliers, stakeholders and other third-party contractors to support the planning and delivery of accessible and \ninclusive visitor experiences across Vivid Sydney. \nThe anticipated locations for these services include: \n? \nSydney CBD \n? \nCircular Quay & The Rocks \n? \nBarangaroo \n? \nDarling Harbour \n? \nOther Vivid Sydney event locations as determined during the planning and review of service stages \nServices will be delivered across the planning, pre-event, event delivery and post-event periods, with onsite \nattendance required at various stages throughout the engagement. \n  \nKey Accountabilities \n\n \n \nRequest for Quote \n6 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nThe Contractor will provide end-to-end accessibility advisory, program administration and onsite support services for \nthe planning and delivery of Vivid Sydney 2027 & 2028. \n \nThe Contractor will be responsible for providing suitably qualified and experienced personnel and sufficient \nresources to deliver all requirements of the Accessibility Program throughout the contracted period. \nAccessibility Advisory & Program Administration \nThe Contractor will: \n? \nProvide strategic and operational accessibility advice to Destination NSW throughout the planning and \ndelivery of Vivid Sydney 2027 & 2028. \n? \nReview key event documentation, operational plans, visitor communications, digital content and other \nrelevant materials and provide practical recommendations to improve accessibility and inclusion outcomes. \n? \nProvide advice on accessibility initiatives, infrastructure, services and visitor experience, informed by \ncontemporary industry best practice for major events and festivals. \n? \nPlan, manage, facilitate and chair the Vivid Sydney Accessibility Advisory Panel. \n? \nReview and, where required, recommend updates to the Panel Terms of Reference and governance \nframework. \n? \nManage the recruitment, contracting and onboarding of new and returning Panel members. \n? \nCoordinate up to seven (7) Panel meetings, including a post-event debrief. \n? \nPrepare and coordinate Panel agendas, papers, minutes, records and ongoing communications. \n? \nCoordinate additional Panel activities, including document reviews, website audits, research and other \nactivities that further the objectives of the Panel. \n? \nManage Panel member financial administration, including invoicing and payments. \n? \nManage individual Panel member Access Riders and coordinate reasonable accessibility requirements \nnecessary to enable participation. \n? \nProvide ongoing communication, advice and support to Panel members throughout the engagement. \n? \nParticipate in relevant planning meetings, workshops and stakeholder consultations as required. \n? \nMaintain appropriate project administration, financial records and reporting. \n? \nCollate Panel findings, observations and recommendations into agreed reporting formats for Destination \nNSW. \nThis component of the Services is anticipated to require up to twenty-five (25) working days across the contracted \nperiod. Services may be delivered as full or partial working days as required. \nOnsite Accessibility Support \nThe Contractor will provide appropriate onsite resources to support the assessment, implementation and delivery of \naccessibility initiatives across the Vivid Sydney event footprint. \nThe Contractor will: \n? \nCoordinate and undertake up to five (5) onsite accessibility assessment visits with the Accessibility Advisory \nPanel. \n? \nUndertake pre-event site visits to review accessibility initiatives against approved event and site plans. \n\n \n \nRequest for Quote \n7 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n? \nIdentify and escalate accessibility issues, discrepancies or outstanding requirements and provide practical \nrecommendations where required. \n? \nSupport the planning, coordination and deployment of accessibility-related signage across the event \nfootprint. \n? \nAssist with the installation and placement of accessibility-related signage where required. \n? \nReview accessibility infrastructure, services, wayfinding and visitor experience from an accessibility and \ninclusion perspective. \n? \nProvide specialist accessibility advice during onsite assessments and pre-event inspections. \n? \nSupport other onsite Accessibility Program activities as reasonably required by Destination NSW. \nThis component of the Services is anticipated to require up to ten (10) working days across the contracted period. \nServices may be delivered as full or partial working days as required. \nThe Contractor must provide their own laptop computer and have an intermediate level of administrative capability, \nincluding proficiency in the Microsoft Office suite. \nSpecific Tasks and General Requirements \nThe Contractor will: \n? \nTake direction from Destination NSW and/or any authorised representative as required. \n? \nWork collaboratively with Destination NSW and relevant third-party contractors, suppliers and stakeholders. \n? \nWork to agreed project schedules, milestones and strict deadlines. \n? \nMaintain sufficient and appropriately skilled personnel to facilitate delivery of the services throughout the \ncontracted period. \n? \nAttend relevant project meetings, site inspections, briefings and operational meetings as required. \n? \nAttend all relevant safety, risk management and site inductions deemed necessary by Destination NSW \nand/or its authorised representatives. \n? \nComply with all relevant Vivid Sydney policies, procedures, WHS requirements and applicable legislative \nrequirements. \n? \nMaintain current knowledge of accessibility and inclusion legislation, standards, guidelines and industry best \npractice relevant to major events and festivals. \n? \nProvide all required project documentation, reports and administrative records within agreed timeframes. \n? \nUndertake any other reasonable administrative or advisory duties required to support delivery of the scope \nof work. \nContractor Resources and Costs \nThe Contractor will be responsible for providing: \n? \nAll suitably qualified and experienced personnel required to deliver the services. \n? \nLaptop computers, mobile phones and other standard equipment required by Contractor personnel. \n? \nAppropriate high-visibility clothing, wet-weather clothing and Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) required \nfor personnel undertaking onsite activities. \n\n \n \nRequest for Quote \n8 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n? \nTravel and other operational costs associated with delivery of the services, unless otherwise expressly \nidentified within the costing template. \n? \nAppropriate administrative support for the Accessibility Advisor and Advisory Panel. \n? \nManagement associated requirements necessary to meet the accessibility needs of Panel members and \npersonnel engaged by the Contractor. \nAccessibility Advisory Panel ? Cost Assumptions \nFor the purpose of pricing, Respondents must base their submission on the following assumptions: \n? \nThe Accessibility Advisory Panel will comprise of up to six (6) Panel members.  \n? \nPanel member fees are to be calculated at $100 per hour, per Panel member (ex GST).  \n? \nPricing is to allow for each Panel member to participate in:  \no \nSix (6) ? two-hour Panel meetings;  \no \nOne (1) ? two-hour post-event debrief; and  \no \nThree (3) ? two-hour onsite assessment visits.  \n? \nThis equates to an allowance of twenty (20) hours per Panel member.  \n? \nRespondents must also include an allowance for reasonable Panel member access requirements and travel \ncosts associated with participation in the program.  \n? \nThe Contractor will be responsible for administering Panel member engagement, including contracting, \ninvoicing, payments and coordination of access and travel requirements. \nParticular Exclusions \nDestination NSW will retain responsibility for the procurement and management of broader event infrastructure, \nequipment and operational suppliers. \nThird-party precincts, venues and activations will remain responsible for the planning and delivery of accessibility \nrequirements within their respective operational areas unless otherwise agreed by Destination NSW. \nThe Contractor's role in relation to these areas will primarily be to assess, advise and make recommendations \nregarding accessibility outcomes rather than directly procure or deliver infrastructure, except where specifically \nidentified within this Scope. \nPeriod: \nDestination NSW requests a rate card for services detailed in this RFQ document, during the period outlined \nbelow: \n \nPeriod 1 \nVivid Sydney 2027 \nOn signing of agreement to completion of Services as \noutlined in the schedule attached  \nEvent Dates \n28 May ? 19 June 2027  \nBump in from \n17 May 2027 (Subject to scheduling)  \nBump out from  \n20 June 2027 (Subject to scheduling)  \n\n \n \nRequest for Quote \n9 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nPeriod 2 \nVivid Sydney 2028 \nOn exercise of the option by Destination NSW and subject \nto execution of any variation to the agreement if \nnecessary. \nEvent Dates \nTo be confirmed \nBump in from  \nTo be confirmed \nBump out from  \nTo be confirmed \n3 \nRFQ Timeline \nRFQ publish date \nThursday, 24 September 2026 \nDeadline for RFQ clarification requests  \n3:00pm, Thursday, 1 October 2026 \nRFQ closing date  \n3:00pm, Thursday, 8 October 2026 \nEvaluation \nMind-October 2026 \nOutcomes communicated \nLate October 2026 \nSuccessful Contractor notified  \nWeek commencing 26 October 2026 \nUnsuccessful Contractor (s) notified  \nWeek commencing 26 October 2026 \nCommencement of Contract  \nMonday, 2 November 2026 \n4 \nSustainability Management and Performance \nThe Supplier must provide all sustainability information reasonably requested by Destination NSW, in the required \nformat and within the specified timeframes, as a condition of this Contract. Failure to comply may delay payment of \ninvoices. \nDestination NSW will provide a pre-event information session on all sustainability information required. \n5 \nOngoing Support and Contract Management \nDestination NSW requires an itemised quotation which addresses the items in the scope. Attention should also be \ndirected towards the Selection Criteria. \n \nRespondents are required to detail their experience and outline their approach to each of the requirements outlined \nin the scope including: \n \n1. Part C1 - A completed response template which addresses the scope outlined above and the selection \ncriteria.    \n2. Part C2 - A completed costing template for period 1 as outlined in the scope, inclusive of all charges.  \n3. Part C3 - Current curriculum vitae including details for two (2) referees. \nClarification of Requests for Quotes:  \nIf you require clarification of this Request for Quotation, please contact the Contact Officer at the details noted \nabove. \n\n \n \nRequest for Quote \n10 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nPlease note that responses to clarifications will be published on the buy.nsw portal after the clarification period for \npublic access.    \n \nHow to respond to a Request for Quotation (RFQ):  \nPlease review the relevant RFQ documentation, Clarification Addendums and Terms & Conditions prior to preparing \nyour response.     \nRespondents should address the specific requirements of the services and, the selection criteria detailed within the \nRFQ documentation. Responses and supporting documentation will need to be uploaded via the NSW Governments \ntender portal at www.buy.nsw.gov.au.   \nNB: This form cannot be saved or changed after submission, please ensure that you check the accuracy of your \ninformation prior to submitting. \n \nDestination NSW will provide: \n? \nRelevant event plans, documentation, schedules and information required to deliver the Services. \n? \nOverarching direction for the Accessibility Advisory Program and Panel. \n? \nAccess to relevant Vivid Sydney personnel, stakeholders and designated event sites as required. \n? \nRelevant site-specific, safety and operational inductions and event access credentials, where applicable. \n? \nLimited onsite parking may be available, subject to operational requirements. Public domain parking is not \nprovided. \n6 \nPricing / Fees & Charges \nThe Supplier is required to complete the attached costing template for period 1 as outlined in the scope,\u202finclusive of \nall charges, known as Part C2. \n \nThe successful contractor must provide: \n? \nAppropriate public liability insurance noting Destination NSW as an Interested Party. Minimum $20 million \n(AUD). \n? \nAppropriate professional liability insurance. Minimum $10 million (AUD). \n? \nAppropriate workers compensation insurance, and any other applicable insurances and accreditations ? as \nrequired by law \n? \nAn Australian Business Number or Australian Company Number. \n? \nHigh quality, guaranteed and fit for purpose Goods and Services \n? \nQualifications, permits, licencing and accreditations relevant to undertake the works detailed herein. Copies \nto be provided to Destination NSW. \n? \nCopies of all relevant Safe Work Method Statements (SWMS). \n? \nCompletion of the Vivid Sydney Risk Management Framework document \n? \nDestination NSW will generate and distribute a sustainability survey post-event, and the Contractor will be \nrequired to submit details on what was used/produced, how much was used/produced and upload \nsupporting files such as receipts, invoices, or bills as evidence. Collection of this information will be tied to \nmilestone payments which will be set out in the contract of engagement with the successful Contractor. \n? \nAny other specific documents as required \n\n \n \nRequest for Quote \n11 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n? \nThe contractor must ensure they adhere to all relevant laws and regulations applicable to the services, \nincluding the Australian Modern Slavery and Child Protection legislation. \n \nThe successful contractor will be required to enter a Contract of Engagement with Destination NSW which includes, \nbut is not limited to the following terms: \n? \nOwnership of all intellectual property (including copyright) in any project material will vest in and/or be \nassigned to Destination NSW on a royalty free, for use worldwide, free of charge in perpetuity basis. The \ncontractor will agree to execute all documents and do all things required for the purposes of giving effect to \nthis. The project material once assigned will be used by Destination NSW, tourism trade stakeholders, media \nand the NSW Government \n? \nIndemnifying Destination NSW for all work carried out. \n? \nAbiding by a supplier?s code of conduct as issued by the NSW Government \n? \nTermination Clause, Destination NSW may at any time terminate the contract without cause and for any \nreason by giving 30 days? notice in writing to the contractor, such termination being effective upon \nexpiration of the 30-day period. Destination NSW is not liable for any payments to the contractor beyond the \n30-day notice period, and such amount will be received by the contractor in full satisfaction and discharge of \nany claims of any nature whatsoever in connection with this contract. Destination NSW would not be liable \nfor any further/pro-rata payments. \n? \nIf the contractor does not perform the services required, the contractor is not entitled to receive any further \npayments and must if so required by notice in writing to the contractor by Destination NSW, repay to \nDestination NSW all payments made under this contract or such part of the payments as so demanded by \nDestination NSW with respect to expenses already incurred and services rendered to date and must make \nsuch repayment within 7 days of the date of such demand by Destination NSW or within such longer period \nas specified by Destination NSW. \n? \nThe use of sub-contractors to deliver parts of this work must include an outline of the proposed contracting \nstructure in response to this Request for Quotation \n? \nThe terms and conditions and deliverables of this request for quotation apply to any sub-contractor/s \nengaged to assist in the delivery of this project. All proposed sub-contractor/s must be approved in writing \nby Destination NSW prior to engagement and be compliant with any relevant legislation regarding payments.  \n? \nAny case studies and public presentations are to be approved by Destination NSW prior to use. \n? \nAny costs incurred in preparing and submitting the RFQ are the responsibility of the Contractor. \n\n"}, {'file_name': 'buy-nsw-tenders-terms-and-conditions-2024.pdf', 'file_type': 'pdf', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/RFQ-12873300325/bd537c4cc2756444-buy-nsw-tenders-terms-and-conditions-2024.pdf', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n1 \n \n \nbuy NSW Tenders module \nGlobal terms and conditions \n \n \nJuly 2024 \n \nGeneral \n1. In using the buy NSW Tenders module you:  \na. agree to terms and conditions contained herein and on buy.nsw.gov.au. \nb. agree that any information you provide is true, accurate, complete, current and not \nmisleading to the best of your knowledge and belief. \nc. agree to act in a way that is ethical, reasonable, with integrity, and complies with \napplicable laws when using the site. \nLodging a response \n2. Responses must be submitted electronically on the buy NSW Tenders module by a registered \nuser of buy NSW. \na. Responses (including all supporting information, if any) must be fully received by the \nclosing date and time. Note: The server date and time will be considered the correct date \nand time for the purposes of tenders closing. All information provided in the Response \nmust be in writing and in English. \nb. Incomplete or corrupted responses that cannot be effectively evaluated may be declined \nor not accepted. \nChanges to an opportunity \n3. An Opportunity may be amended before the closing date and time by issuing an amendment \nnotice. The amendment notice becomes part of the Opportunity.  \n4. It is the responsibility of all respondents: \na. to verify if an amendment notice has been issued prior to the closing date and time by \nchecking buy.nsw.gov.au, \nb. if necessary, respond to the amendment notice even if a Response has already been \nsubmitted by the Respondent.  \nQuestion and answers \n5. The buy nsw Tenders module includes question and answer functionality. You can submit a \nquestion to clarify tender requirements. Unless an amendment is issued the answer provided by \nthe Buyer is not considered part of the Opportunity. \n Use of responses \n6. In lodging a Response, you agree that the information provided: \n\na. can be used, shared and copied (including providing access to any third parties) for \nany purpose related to this Opportunity, and for internal procurement purposes. \nb. can be made available, on request, to any NSW government agency. This includes \ninformation dealing with the Respondent?s performance on any prior contract that has \nbeen awarded. Such information may be used by the recipient NSW Government \nagency for reasons including, but not limited to, the assessment of the suitability for \npre-qualification, selective tender lists, expressions of interest or the award of a \ncontract. \nc. is communication falling within section 30 of the Defamation Act 2005 (NSW). You shall \nhave no claim against the Principal and the State of New South Wales in respect of \nany matter arising out of the provision or receipt of such information, including any \nclaim for loss arising out of the communication. \nData security \n7. We maintain appropriate physical, technical, and administrative security to help prevent the loss, \nmisuse, unauthorised access, disclosure or modification of data on\u202fthe site. We do not accept any \nliability for the loss, misuse, unauthorised access, disclosure or modification of your data. \n8. To protect your data all Responses are encrypted at rest and in transit. Responses cannot be \ndecrypted until the close date and time. Responses can only be decrypted by the tender owner \nthrough the tender box opening process.  \nCorruption or unethical conduct \n9. A response may be declined or not considered if a Respondent, or any of its officers, employees, \nagents or sub-contractors is found to have: \na. offered any inducement, rebate, uncommercial/inflated pricing or reward to any \npublic servant or employee, agent or subcontractor of the Principal or the NSW \nGovernment in connection with this RFQ or the submitted Quotation; or \nb. committed corrupt conduct within the meaning of the Independent Commission \nAgainst Corruption Act 1988; or \nc. a record or alleged record of unethical behaviour. \n10. The Principal may, in its discretion, invite a relevant Respondent to provide written comments \nwithin a specified time before the Principal excludes the Respondent from the RFQ under clause \n10. \nNSW Government Procurement Policy Framework \n11. In submitting a response, the Respondent agrees to comply with the NSW Government \nProcurement Policy Framework (available at www.buy.nsw.gov.au).  \n12. If any Respondent fails to comply with these requirements, the Principal may take such failure \ninto account when considering the response and any subsequent response submitted by the \nRespondent and may result in the response being rejected. \nComplaints procedure \n13. It is the NSW Government?s objective to ensure that industry is given every opportunity to win \nGovernment contracts. Should any entity feel that it has been unfairly excluded from quotation \nor unfairly disadvantaged by the Agreement or the Statement of Requirements, it is invited to \nwrite to the Principal \n14. If a complaint is received concerning an alleged breach of an enforceable procurement provision \nunder Division 5 of Part 11 of the Public Works and Procurement Act 1912, this opportunity will be \nsuspended in accordance with the statutory requirements and invited Respondents will be \nnotified accordingly. \n\nBreaches to terms \n15. Respondents and users who breach the terms will be warned by the site?s administrator. \nSubsequent breaches will result in the user being blocked from accessing the site and legal \naction may be taken. \n16. If deemed necessary, breaches to terms may result in the termination of user accounts \n(including Supplier profiles), without liability for any loss. Once we end your user account, all \nrights granted to you in these terms will cease. \nJurisdiction \n17. The laws of New South Wales, Australia govern these terms. Any dispute must be heard in the \ncourts of New South Wales. \n18. If any of these terms are found to be invalid, illegal or unenforceable or for any reason by the \ncourts of New South Wales, the remaining terms will continue to apply in full. \n\n'}, {'file_name': '20260917-DNSW-Contract-of-Engagement-Template.docx', 'file_type': 'docx', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/RFQ-12873300325/5296ee3175415465-20260917-DNSW-Contract-of-Engagement-Template.docx', 'extracted_text': 'PARTY DETAILS\nParties\nDestination NSW\nLevel 2, 88 Cumberland Street, The Rocks, NSW, 2000\nABN: 52 890 768 976\n(Destination NSW or DNSW)\nand\n[Supplier name]\n[Insert registered address]\nABN/ACN/International Business Number \n(Supplier)\nCONTRACT SCHEDULE \nEXECUTION:\nCONTRACT STANDARD TERMS AND CONDITIONS\nGoods and Services\nFrom the Commencement Date until the Completion Date, the Supplier is engaged to undertake, supply and provide the goods and/or services, duties and outcomes set out in Item 3 and Item 4 of the Contract Schedule (?Goods and/or Services?). In exchange for the Goods and/or Services Destination NSW will pay the Supplier the Contract Price as set out in Item 6 of the Contract Schedule upon receipt of a valid invoice as set out in Item 7. \nEach party states that:\nit has full power and authority to execute, delivery and perform its obligations under this Contract;\nthis Contract is legally binding on and enforceable against it;\nit will comply with all applicable laws in the performance of its obligations under this Contract;\nits signatories are properly authorised; and\nit can enter into this Contract without breaching any other contract or legal obligation to anyone else.\nDestination NSW will reimburse the Supplier for out-of-pocket expenses in connection with Services, if any have been so identified for reimbursement in the invoice, provided that the Supplier has obtained prior written approval to incur such expenses at Destination NSW?s expense before they are incurred and valid tax invoices and receipts are provided for reconciliation purposes in accordance with Item 7. \nThe Supplier must provide the Goods and/or Services in accordance with any Milestones or Delivery Dates set out in Item 5 of the Contract Schedule. \nWhere the Supplier is supplying Goods to Destination NSW it:\nmust ensure and warrant that title to the Goods passes to Destination NSW on the Delivery Date free from encumbrances and all other adverse interests;\nacknowledges that all risk in the Goods will remain the Supplier\'s until the Goods are installed or delivered to the satisfaction of Destination NSW; and\nwarrants that the goods will be: \nfree from defects in materials or workmanship; \nof merchantable quality; \nfit for purpose; and,\ncarry any applicable manufacturer?s warranty which will pass to Destination NSW.\nThe Supplier and any Key Person/s must not unreasonably refuse a request by Destination NSW to inspect and test the Goods prior to acceptance of delivery of the Goods. Destination NSW is entitled to reject any Goods which are not manufactured or delivered in accordance with the terms of this Contract (which includes a failure to comply with the warranties contained in clause 5 (c) of this Contract). Destination NSW is not deemed to have accepted any Goods until the latter of:\nthe expiration of a reasonable time for inspection of the Goods following delivery and installation; or\nany latent defect in the Goods becoming apparent. \nIf any Goods are rejected by Destination NSW under clause 6, title to and risk in those Goods automatically re-vests in the Supplier. At the Supplier\'s sole expense, it is responsible for the collection and removal of the rejected Goods. Destination NSW may, in its sole discretion, require the Supplier to replace any rejected Goods, or reimburse the costs arising from having to acquire replacement Goods from a third party, without prejudice to any of the other rights or remedies Destination NSW may have under this Contract or at law. \nWhere the Supplier is providing Services to Destination NSW, the Supplier and any Key Person/s must:\nperform the Services in accordance with best industry practice. Destination NSW relies on the Supplier, as the Supplier of the Services, to know what the best industry practice is;\ncomply with the laws, standards and policies that apply to the Services;\nperform the Services with the due care, skill and diligence expected of a professional service supplier and in a proper and workmanlike manner; \nperform the Services in a cost-effective manner consistent with the required level of quality of performance; and\nensure that any equipment used in delivering the Services is suitable and in good, safe and serviceable order and continuously maintained or repaired so that it remains in such order.\nThe Supplier must notify Destination NSW immediately if it anticipates that there may be a delay in the provision of the Goods and/or Services. Any request for an extension of time to deliver the Goods and/or Services requires Destination NSW?s approval, which will not be unreasonably withheld. \nThe Supplier agrees that any work performed for Destination NSW will be deemed to fall within the Services for the Contract Price set out in Item 6 of the Contract Schedule. Should the Supplier wish to perform work believed to be outside the scope of the Services, it must seek written approval from Destination NSW before proceeding with any such work. Such approval may be withheld by Destination NSW in its sole discretion. \nTerm\nThis Contract will commence on the Commencement Date identified in Item 1 and will continue until the Completion Date identified in Item 1, unless renewed in accordance with clause 12.   \nThe Parties agree that Destination NSW may elect to renew this Contract for the Option Period(s) set out in Item 1 of the Contract Schedule, subject to the consent of the Supplier. \nIf Destination NSW elects to exercise an option to renew, Destination NSW will provide, within 30 days before the expiration of the Completion Date, notice in writing to the Supplier and the Contract Price Review mechanism set out in Item 8 of the Contract Schedule will apply. Unless otherwise agreed in writing between the parties (email is sufficient), all other terms and conditions set out in this Contract will remain in full force and effect.  \nInvoice and Tax\nThe Supplier must submit a tax invoice at the timeframes set out in Item 7 of the Contract Schedule. \nDestination NSW may withhold or apply a set-off against any tax invoice submitted by the Supplier if Destination NSW is not satisfied, acting reasonably, that the Goods and/or Services have been provided in accordance with the terms and conditions of this Contract. \nOther than as provided in this Contract, all taxes, duties and charges and taxation obligations imposed or levied in connection to the Contract Price (including, without limitation, making appropriate tax deductions and payments in respect of any Key Person/s as applicable) will be borne by the Supplier. \nIf GST is applicable under or in connection with this Contract, any reference in this clause to a term defined or used in the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999 (Cth) (?Act?) is, unless the context indicates otherwise, a reference to that term as defined or used in the Act. \nAny amount referred to in this Contract except as indicated otherwise, is expressed on a GST exclusive basis. The Supplier takes all responsibility for determining whether GST is applicable to it and Destination NSW will not be liable whatsoever, for upholding the Supplier\'s requirements and obligations under the Act. \nIf GST is payable under or in connection with this Contract, the consideration to be provided for the supply (unless it is expressly stated to include GST) is increased by an amount equal to the GST exclusive consideration multiplied by the rate at which GST is imposed in respect of the supply. \nTermination \nDestination NSW may at any time terminate this Contract for convenience by giving the Supplier 30 days? notice in writing, such termination being effective upon expiration of the 30-day period. Destination NSW will not be liable for any payments to the Supplier over and above the value of the payment due and payable for Goods provided or Services performed up to the date of termination under this clause. \nEither party may terminate this Contract if the other commits a material breach of this Contract which is either not capable of being remedied or which the party committing the breach has not remedied within seven (7) (or such larger number as agreed in writing) days of receiving a written notice requiring remedy from the other party. \nDestination NSW may terminate this Contract where the Supplier becomes bankrupt or stops, suspends or threatens to stop payment of all or a class of its debts, or where the Supplier appoints an administrator or liquidator.\nUpon termination of this Contract, any property belonging to Destination NSW which is in the possession of the Supplier and its Key Person/s must be immediately returned to Destination NSW.\nWhere this Contract is terminated by Destination NSW under clauses 21 or 22, Destination NSW may withhold any or all of the fees payable to the Supplier in exchange for the Services (other than fees for Goods or Services provided prior to the date of termination). Any fees prepaid by Destination NSW to the Supplier for Goods or Services not delivered to Destination NSW by the termination date are to be refunded to Destination NSW no later than 10 business days after the termination date.    \nIf there is any actual or threatened conduct which is or may be a breach of this Contract, both parties acknowledge that damages may be inadequate compensation for such a breach. Either party will be entitled to apply to any court of competent jurisdiction for interim or permanent injunctive relief or both, restraining the other party from committing any breach or threatened breach of this Contract. Such rights and remedies will be cumulative and in addition to any other rights or remedies which either party may be entitled to at law or in equity. \nForce Majeure\nA party (?Affected Party?) will not be liable for any failure or delay in the performance of its obligations under this Contract to the extent that such failure or delay is caused, directly or indirectly by any of the following circumstances (?Force Majeure Circumstance?):\nfire, flood, earthquake, or acts of God;\nstrikes or industrial action (but not strikes or industrial action by any employees of Destination NSW or the Supplier); and\nacts of war, pandemic, epidemic, terrorism, riots, civil disorders or rebellions or revolutions in Australia. \nThe Affected Party must:\npromptly advise the other party of the details of the Force Majeure Circumstance, and its likely effect on the Affected Party?s obligations under this Contract; and\ntake all steps reasonably necessary to recommence performance and minimise the delay caused by the Force Majeure Circumstance. \nIf the Services cannot be rearranged so that they are conducted, the Supplier will refund to Destination NSW within 10 business days of receiving notice from Destination NSW any unspent portion of the payments and no further payments will be made. \nIndemnity\nThe Supplier must indemnify Destination NSW against any loss, action, proceedings, demand, prosecution, judgment, damage, cost, expense, fine, penalty or liability claimed against or incurred by Destination NSW in relation to:\npersonal injury or damage to property arising out of:\na wilful misconduct (being an act or omission which was a deliberate and wrongful act or omission or involved reckless disregard or wanton indifference to the likely consequences) or any negligent act or omission by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s, to provide the Goods or Services; or\nany breach by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s of this Contract (including but not limited to any loss resulting from the Supplier not being registered for GST and ABN purposes);\nbreach by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s, of applicable Privacy and Data Protection Laws or confidentiality obligations; or\na material breach of the Contract by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s, or infringement of Destination NSW?s intellectual property rights by the Supplier or its Key Peron/s; and,\nthe Supplier and/or its Key Person/s claiming that they are Destination NSW\'s employee, including claims for wages, superannuation, public holidays, annual leave, parental leave, personal carer s leave, long service leave, redundancy or termination of employment or any other employment entitlement. \nThe indemnity in this clause 29 will not apply to the extent that the relevant loss, action, proceedings, demand, prosecution, judgment, damage, cost, expense, fine, penalty or liability is caused or contributed to by Destination NSW.  \nKey Persons\nIf any Key Person/s are named at Item 2 of the Contract Schedule, then the Supplier must ensure that those Key Person/s are fully engaged in the Services on behalf of the Supplier. If any Key Person is unavailable, then the Supplier must notify Destination NSW and provide, at the earliest opportunity, replacements with comparable skill and experience, subject to written approval by Destination NSW. The Suppler acknowledges and agrees that Destination NSW may, in its absolute discretion, reject any proposed replacement(s) of one or more Key Person/s and that where this occurs, the Supplier must provide an alternate person with comparable skill and experience approved by Destination NSW of as soon as possible.\nConfidentiality, Personal Information and GIPA\nSubject to the Government Information (Public Access) Act 2009 (NSW) (GIPA Act), the Supplier and its Key Person/s acknowledge the importance of confidentiality in all aspects of Destination NSW?s operations and agree to keep all Confidential Information in absolute confidence and not to disclose any such information to any person without our prior written consent both during and after the term of this Contract. \n"Confidential Information" in this Contract includes any information disclosed by Destination NSW or by its employees, agents and assigns to the Supplier either before or after the commencement of the Term that is confidential by its nature, is designated as confidential, specifically identified as confidential, or the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s know, or ought to know, is confidential. \nBoth parties may disclose Confidential Information to their need-to-know employees and agents and to their legal and professional advisors to the extent required for the performance of this Contract, subject to an ongoing obligation of confidentiality and provided that the disclosing party ensures that the recipient in turn keeps the Confidential Information confidential and does not use the information except for the purposes of this Contract. Your The Supplier\'s intended recipients must be pre-approved in writing by Destination NSW and you the Supplier will ensure that its permitted recipients of our Confidential Information execute a confidentiality deed, prior to receiving the Confidential Information, to protect Destination NSW, which deed must be in a form approved by Destination NSW. \nBoth parties must collect, use, and store any Personal Information in relation to the Services strictly in compliance with the Privacy and Data Protection Laws and: \nFor the purpose of the clause, Privacy and Data Protection Laws means the Privacy and Personal Information Protection Act 1998 (NSW) and if relevant to the performance of this Contract, the Health Records and Information Privacy Act 2002 (NSW), the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), the Spam Act 2003 (Cth), the General Data Protection Regulation (2016/679), and the Privacy and Electronic Communications Directive (2002/58/EC), including any principles under those Acts and codes of practice relating to those Acts.\nFor the purpose of this clause, "Personal Information" means the definition set out in the relevant Privacy and Data Protection Laws. \nImmediately on termination of this Contract for any reason, both parties must:\ncease the use of all Confidential Information of, or relating to, the other party;\ndeliver to the other party all documents and other materials in its possession or control containing, recording or constituting the other party?s Confidential Information, or, at the other party?s option, destroy, and certify to the other party that it has destroyed, those documents; \nfor other party Confidential Information stored electronically, permanently delete that Confidential Information from all electronic media on which it is stored, so that it cannot be restored.\nNothing in clause 35 above shall prevent Destination NSW from any record storage actions it is required to take pursuant to the State Records Act 1998 (NSW).  \nThe Supplier acknowledges that the restrictions in this clause are reasonable in all the circumstances and necessary to protect the assets and information of Destination NSW (including brand and reputation) and the remedy of damages may be inadequate to protect the interests of Destination NSW and Destination NSW is entitled to seek and obtain injunctive relief, or any other relief.\nRight of Access to Records and Information\nFor the purposes of assisting Destination NSW meet its obligations under the GIPA Act, the Supplier must, within seven (7) days of receiving a written request by Destination NSW, provide Destination NSW with immediate access to the following information contained in records held by the Supplier or its Key Person/s:\ninformation that relates directly to the provision to Destination NSW of the Goods or Services under this Contract;\ninformation collected by the Supplier from members of the public for the purposes of the provision of the Goods or Services under this Contract; and/or\ninformation received by the Supplier from Destination NSW to enable the Supplier to provide the Goods or Services under this Contract. \nFor the purpose of clause 38, information does not include:\ninformation that discloses or would tend to disclose financing arrangements, financial modelling, cost structure or profit margin;\ninformation that the Supplier is prohibited from disclosing to Destination NSW by provision made by or under any act, whether of any State or Territory, or of the Commonwealth; or\ninformation that, if disclosed to Destination NSW, could reasonably be expected to place the Supplier at a substantial commercial disadvantage in relation to Destination NSW, whether at present or in the future. \nThe Supplier will provide copies of any of the information requested by Destination NSW under clause 38 and any associated costs for this may be separately agreed between the parties. \nAny failure by the Supplier to comply with any request under clause 38 above will be considered a breach of an essential term and will allow Destination NSW to terminate this Contract by providing notice in writing of its intention to do so with the termination to take effect seven (7) days after receipt of the notice. If the Supplier fails to remedy the breach within the seven (7) day period to the satisfaction of Destination NSW, then the termination will take effect seven (7) days after receipt of the notice.\nIntellectual Property \nThe Supplier agrees that ownership of all intellectual property rights subsisting in any material created under this Contract, including but not limited to [insert specific description of relevant material under this Contract, or delete] plans, documents and photographs or footage (final and raw, proofs, negatives) shall vest in Destination NSW royalty free, for use worldwide, free of charge and in perpetuity. For the purpose of this clause, ?intellectual property? expressly excludes the Supplier?s pre-existing materials, and the Supplier retains all right, title and interest in and to any and all pre-existing materials. For the purposes of this Contract, the pre-existing materials are deemed to be the Supplier?s confidential information. To the extent any pre-existing materials are used in connection with the Services and/or incorporated or contained in any deliverable provided to Destination NSW pursuant to this contract, the Supplier hereby grants Destination NSW a non-exclusive, irrevocable, perpetual, royalty-free, worldwide license to use the pre-existing materials in accordance with this contract. The Supplier?s pre-existing materials include any software, tools, algorithms, know-how, ideas, techniques, materials, architecture, methodologies, concepts, trade secrets and other proprietary information that were owned or licensed by the Supplier prior to entering into this Contract. The Supplier warrants that Destination NSW?s exploitation of the intellectual property rights referenced in this clause, as contemplated under this Contract, will not infringe the intellectual property rights of any third party. \nDestination NSW intellectual property subsisting in any materials or documents provided by Destination NSW to the Supplier is the sole and exclusive property of Destination NSW or its licensors (as applicable) and this Contract does not confer on the Supplier any proprietary right, ownership, or title to any of the Destination NSW intellectual property. \nThe Supplier consents to and must ensure each of its employee(s) and contractor(s) consent to Destination NSW doing anything in respect of the intellectual property rights assigned or licensed to Destination NSW under this Contract which would otherwise breach the moral rights of the affected party granted to it by the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). \nDispute Resolution\nIf a dispute under this Contract arises (?Dispute?):\nthe party claiming that a Dispute has arisen must give a written notice to the other party (?Dispute Notice?), setting out reasonable particulars of the Dispute;\nwithin five (5) business days after receipt of a Dispute Notice, a senior representative of each party must meet and attempt to resolve the Dispute within 10 business days of receipt of the Dispute Notice;\nif the parties fail to meet or resolve the Dispute within 10 business days of the receipt of the Dispute Notice, the chief executive officer (or equivalent) of each party (or his or her nominee) must meet (in person or by way of telephone conference) and attempt to resolve the Dispute within 20 business days of receipt of the Dispute Notice; and\nif the parties fail to meet or resolve the Dispute within 20 business days of receipt of the Dispute Notice, the parties may take whatever action they consider necessary to resolve the Dispute. \nCommunications between the parties during the process set out in clause 45, whether oral or in writing, will not be admissible as evidence in any legal process unless in writing and signed by both parties. \nDespite the existence of a Dispute, the parties must continue to perform their respective obligation under this Contract. \nNothing in clauses 45 to 47 prevents a party issuing proceedings from obtaining urgent injunctive or urgent declaratory relief. \nAssignment\nThe Supplier may, with Destination NSW?s approval, assign any rights or duties under this Contract or sub-contract performance of this Contract or any part of it. Provided the individual or entity the Supplier assigns or subcontracts any rights or duties to is qualified to perform the Services, Destination NSW?s approval will not be unreasonably withheld. Engaging sub-contractors to provide any part of the Services will not relieve the Supplier from any of its liabilities or obligations under this Contract. \nArtificial Intelligence Systems\nNotwithstanding any other provision of this Contract, each party (Party A) must not use or deploy an Artificial Intelligence System in the performance of this Contract, and is prohibited, without the other party?s (Party B) prior written consent, from reproducing, publishing, communicating, or otherwise using, in whole or in part:\nany intellectual property, confidential information or any other information of Party B; or \nany material created under this contract by either party; or\nor any trademarks of Party B; \n(collectively Party B Material),\nto develop, train or direct, Artificial Intelligence Systems, technology or models, including the mining or scraping of text, images or data from Party B Material that vests in whole or in part in Party B whether undertaken by the Party A or by third parties authorised, licensed or directed by Party A.\nArtificial Intelligence System means a model, or a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments.\nModern Slavery\nFor the purpose of this clause:\nCore Obligations means those obligations set out in clause 53 (Core Obligations);\nEngaged Entity of a party means any first tier (direct) suppliers, subcontractors, consultants and contractors engaged by that party (or that party?s directors, officers and employees) in connection with this Contract. For the avoidance of doubt, ?Engaged Entities? includes independent contractors (whether an individual or body corporate), secondees, consultants and any other workers (however described) who may be engaged for the purposes of this Contract but are not employed by the relevant party;\nManagement Plan means a plan to take reasonable steps to manage risks of Modern Slavery in the Supplier?s operations and supply chains (including in the operations and supply chains of Supplier?s Engaged Entities);\nMaterial Breach has the meaning given in clause 58;\nModern Slavery:\nmeans any conduct that constitutes or would constitute any offence listed in Schedule 2 of the Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW), including an offence of attempting or incitement to commit such an offence;\nincludes any conduct that constitutes or would constitute an offence under any of the Modern Slavery Laws as amended from time to time, including an offence of attempting or incitement to commit such an offence; and\nincludes conduct engaged in elsewhere than in New South Wales that, if it occurred in New South Wales, would constitute a modern slavery offence under paragraphs (i) or (ii);\nModern Slavery Laws means:\nthe Modern Slavery Act 2018 (Cth);\nthe Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW);\nDivisions 270 and 271 of the Commonwealth Criminal Code;\nsection 176(1A) of the Public Works and Procurement Act 1912 (NSW);\nsection 438ZE of the Local Government Act 1993 (NSW); and\nany other laws, regulations, codes and international conventions aimed at combatting modern slavery, forced labour or human trafficking, from time to time in force in or ratified by Australia and, where relevant, in or by other jurisdictions in which the parties operate, \neach as amended from time to time;\nreasonable steps means those steps that are reasonable in the circumstances to prevent, identify, mitigate and remedy modern slavery. In assessing whether steps are reasonable, the parties may refer to the NSW Anti-slavery Commissioner?s Guidance on Reasonable Steps and related information and resources published by the Anti-slavery Commissioner;\nRelated Body Corporate has the meaning given to that term in the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth);\nRelated Entity means, in respect of a party, a Related Body Corporate of such party;\nRemediation Plan has the meaning given to it in clause 59. \nThe Supplier must:\nnot engage in Modern Slavery;\ntake reasonable steps to ensure that its directors, officers, employees, Related Entities and Engaged Entities comply with Modern Slavery Laws as applicable;\ntake reasonable steps to ensure that its Engaged Entities include provisions equivalent to the Core Obligations (including this sub-clause) in their contracts with their suppliers; and\ntake reasonable steps to ensure that its Engaged Entities provide their respective directors, officers, employees and suppliers with at least the minimum level of wages and other entitlements required by law. \nThe Supplier acknowledges and agrees that the Contract Price supports the Supplier to comply with its Core Obligations.\nThe Supplier agrees that it will establish, implement, and maintain for the term of this Contract, appropriate systems and policies as required to meet its Core Obligations.\nWithout limiting the Core Obligations and clause 55, and to the extent permitted by law, the Supplier agrees that it will notify Destination NSW immediately with adequate particulars of the Modern Slavery and the actions taken, or being taken, to remedy the Modern Slavery if the Supplier becomes aware of any actual or reasonably suspected Modern Slavery engaged in, or any notices, investigations, proceedings or claims arising in any jurisdiction in relation to any actual or reasonably suspected breach of Modern Slavery Laws by the Supplier, the Supplier\'s directors, officers, employees, Related Entities, or by any of its Engaged Entities, whether or not the Modern Slavery occurs or is suspected to occur in the performance of the Contract.\nThe Supplier represents and warrants to, and for the benefit of, Destination NSW that, as at the Contract Date and on a continuing basis for the duration of the term of this Contract, the Supplier has disclosed, in accordance with clause 56:\nto the extent the Supplier is aware, any:\nactual or reasonably suspected Modern Slavery engaged in; and\nnotices, investigations, proceedings or claims arising in any jurisdiction in relation to any actual or reasonably suspected breach of Modern Slavery Laws,\nby the Supplier, he Supplier\'s directors, officers, employees, or Related Entities, or by any of the Supplier?s Engaged Entities while performing any contract with the Supplier, whether or not the Modern Slavery arises in the performance of the Contract; and\nall actions taken to remedy said Modern Slavery or breach of Modern Slavery Laws. \nWithout limiting the parties? rights under this Contract, the parties agree that:\na breach of the Supplier?s obligations under this clause will be taken to be a Material Breach of this Contract; and\nbefore exercising any termination rights that may arise as a result of this Material Breach, the parties shall consult with relevant stakeholders on whether Modern Slavery may arise from such termination and the reasonable steps to prevent or mitigate such risk of Modern Slavery. \nWhere Destination NSW forms the view that there is a Material Breach that is reasonably capable of being remedied, then the parties shall develop a remediation plan to take reasonable steps to remedy the breach in accordance with this Contract (the Remediation Plan). The Supplier shall take reasonable efforts to implement this Remediation Plan. \nPublic Interest Disclosures\nIn clauses 60 to 67, words and expressions:\nwhich are not defined in this Contract, but which have a defined meaning in the PID Act, have the same meaning as in the PID Act; and\nwhich are defined in this Contract but, by such a definition, are given a different meaning in other clauses of this Contract to the meaning given in the PID Act, take the same meaning as in the PID Act. \nThe Supplier must ensure that all individuals involved in providing services under this Contract are made aware of the following:\nthat those individuals are public officials for the purposes of the PID Act;\nhow to make a voluntary public interest disclosure;\nDestination NSW?s public interest disclosure policy; and\nthe fact that a person who is dissatisfied with the way in which a voluntary public interest disclosure has been dealt with may be entitled to take further action under the PID Act or another Act or law.\nThe Supplier must notify Destination NSW of a voluntary public interest disclosure of which the Supplier becomes aware where either:\nthe disclosure relates to Destination NSW; or\nthe maker of the disclosure is known to be a public official associated with Destination NSW.\nThe Supplier must notify Destination NSW of serious wrongdoing committed, or alleged to be committed, by an individual providing services under this Contract.\nThe Supplier must use its best endeavours to assist in an investigation of serious wrongdoing if requested to do so by a person dealing with a voluntary public disclosure on behalf of Destination NSW or any other agency (as defined in the PID Act).\nThe Supplier acknowledges that:\nDestination NSW has an obligation to take corrective action under the PID Act; and\nDestination NSW has a right to terminate the Contract in response to a finding of serious wrongdoing or other misconduct involving the Supplier or an individual providing services under this Contract.\nIf the Supplier subcontracts the Contract in whole or in part, the Supplier must ensure that the subcontract contains terms binding the person or body engaged under the subcontract that are equivalent to the terms binding the Supplier in clauses 60 to 67.\nThe parties acknowledge that the regulations made under the PID Act may make further provision about terms that must, or must not, be included in an agency service contract or a class of agency service contracts.\nEntire agreement and previous representations\nThis Contract states all the express terms of the Contract between the parties and supersedes all prior discussions and agreements in respect of its subject matter. \nUpon entering this Contract, the Supplier will be taken to have investigated and satisfied itself of the adequacy of all information relied upon by the Supplier to form a position in relation to the Goods or Services to be provided under this Contract. \nTo the maximum extent permitted by law, and other than as expressly specified in this Contract, Destination NSW does not:\nmake any representation or warranty (express or implied) as to the accuracy, completeness, currency, fitness for purpose, quality, reliability, usefulness or timeliness of the content of the information relied upon by the Supplier in entering this Contract to provide the Goods and/or Services or as to any of Destination NSW?s Confidential Information provided to the Supplier; or\nhave any liability whatsoever for any decision made by the Supplier or any other person to engage with Destination NSW to provide the Goods and/or Services. \nInsurance\nThe Supplier must take out and maintain the insurance cover identified at Item 10 of the Schedule with a reputable insurance company for the period of this Contract. Destination NSW may request confirmation of such cover by way of certificates of currency prior to the commencement of the Term. \nThe Supplier is also solely responsible for ensuring that any sub-contractors engaged are also suitably insured and acknowledges that it and its Key Person/s are not entitled to the benefit of any accident, third party, public liability or indemnity policies of insurance or any workers\' compensation policies which may be in force for the benefit or protection of Destination NSW staff. \nThe Supplier must roll over insurance policies as they expire to ensure coverage for the duration of the Term. If the Supplier fails to acquire adequate insurance or fail to renew its insurance policies during any roll over period within the Term, the Supplier must notify Destination NSW immediately.  Evidence of insurances, by way of certificates of currency, are to be provided to Destination NSW upon request.\nRelationship\nIt is agreed that the relationship created by this Contract is that of an independent contractor(s) and nothing contained in this Contract shall be deemed or construed as creating any partnership, joint venture, employment relationship, agency or other relationship between the parties or to make Destination NSW liable for the debts or obligations of the Supplier. Consequently, the Supplier agrees by entering into this Contract that they are:\nnot entitled to payment of salary, holiday pay, severance pay, long service leave, superannuation, ongoing employment or any other entitlement which an employee has in respect of their employer;\nsolely responsible for all payments in relation to taxes;\nsolely responsible for providing superannuation, leave and work cover in respect of its Key Person/s; and\nsolely responsible for complying with all relevant laws and regulations relating to the provision of this clause. \nConflicts of Interest\nThe Supplier must take reasonable steps to ensure that it (and its Key Person/s, employees, agents and assigns) are not subject to any conflict of interest that would adversely affect its ability to supply the Services to Destination NSW in accordance with the Contract or be competitive with Destination NSW or adversely impact upon Destination NSW\'s interests. Where there is potentially any conflict of interest, the Supplier must promptly inform Destination NSW. If the conflict of interest is of substantial concern to Destination NSW it may, in its absolute discretion, terminate this Contract under clause 20. \nDisrepute and no public comment\nThe Supplier agrees, covenants and warrants that it will not act in any way or manner which is, in the reasonable opinion of Destination NSW, prejudicial or otherwise detrimental to Destination NSW. The Supplier further agrees that it will not make any adverse, critical or disparaging statements, allegations or comments (including on social media) which disparage Destination NSW, the NSW Premier, the NSW Government or the Minister for Jobs and Tourism (or relevant portfolio Minister if there is a change to Ministerial title). The Supplier agrees to always use all reasonable efforts to preserve the goodwill and reputation of Destination NSW and comply with any reasonable requirement imposed by Destination NSW from time to time. The Supplier agrees it will use best endeavours to preserve and protect the value and validity of Destination NSW?s intellectual property, including by providing all necessary support, information, materials and assistance to enable Destination NSW to maintain and promote its image and reputation.\nUnless expressly agreed in writing by Destination NSW, the Supplier must not engage in any public comment or communication (including comments on radio, television, social media and digital platforms, comments to a journalist, and comments expressed in letters, newspapers, books, journals and notices) concerning the Services and your engagement by Destination NSW, or issue any public statement or communication or make any representation that in any way directly or indirectly affects Destination NSW or its affairs. Under no circumstances are there to be any media announcements (including exclusives) or information shared with the media without Destination NSW?s express approval. Furthermore, there are to be no media or press releases issued, including those under embargo, nor any pre briefing of any media.\nGeneral\n(Severability) If any part of this Contract is invalid, illegal or unenforceable, that part will be severed from this Contract without affecting the continued operation of the remainder of this Contract, and the rest of the Contract is enforceable except where enforcement would materially change the nature or effect of the parties? obligations under this Agreement.\n(Notices) Notices are to be sent by post, email or hand delivered to the attention of the respective persons referred to at Item 9 of the Contract Schedule at the address recorded there (or as notified in writing to the other party). \n(Jurisdiction) This Contract is governed by and in accordance with the laws in the State of New South Wales. The parties expressly submit to the exclusive jurisdiction of the courts in the state of NSW and courts of appeal from them. The Supplier irrevocably waives any objection to the venue of any legal process in these courts as being in an inconvenient forum. \n(Novation) Destination NSW may at any time require you to be a party to a novation of this Contract to any other NSW Government agency and you must consent to such novation and execute any document reasonably required by Destination NSW to give effect to it. \n(Counterparts) This Contract may be executed in any number of counterparts and scanned copies may be valid.\n(Survival) Clauses 20 to 25 inclusive (termination), 29 (indemnity), 31 to 48 inclusive (confidentiality), 38 to 41 inclusive (right of access to records), 42 to 44 inclusive (intellectual property), 45 to 48 inclusive (dispute resolution), 49 (assignment), 50 to 51 inclusive (artificial intelligence systems), 68 to 70 inclusive (entire agreement and previous representations), 76 (non-disrepute), 77 (no public comment), 80 (jurisdiction); 81 (novation) continue to operate and survive the termination or expiry of this Contract. Termination of this Contract does not affect the rights of Destination NSW against the Supplier in respect of anything done or omitted to be done by the Supplier under this Contract before termination or regarding any sums or other claims outstanding at the time of termination.\nANNEXURES\nThe documents below are subject to clauses 1 and 2 of the Contract Standard Terms and Conditions and form part of this contract:\n\n\nANNEXURE A ? SPECIFICATION OF SERVICES\n'}, {'file_name': 'Part-C2---VS-2027-RFQ--Accessibility-Advisor---Costing-Template.xlsx', 'file_type': 'other', 'storage_uri': 'gs://tenderai-dev-documents/tenders/buynsw/RFQ-12873300325/f2cded856e1e58d5-Part-C2---VS-2027-RFQ--Accessibility-Advisor---Costing-Template.xlsx', 'extracted_text': None}, {'file_name': 'Part-C1---VS27-Request-for-Quotation---Accessibility-Advisor---Response-Template.docx', 'file_type': 'docx', 's